## Summary

Addresses the hang reported in #29594. The `google_web_search` tool waits for
its model request with no time limit. If the request stalls, or the server
keeps returning retryable errors, the agent stays on that one tool call for
minutes, or indefinitely, until the user cancels it.

Note: #29594 was closed by its reporter before a fix landed; the Nov 7 retest on
that thread confirmed that `WebSearchTool` on `main` still imposes no overall
deadline across retries.

This PR limits one web search to 60 seconds, retries included. At the limit the
tool returns a normal tool error, so the agent can carry on.

## Details

- `tools/web-search.ts`: a local `AbortController` + `setTimeout` forwards
  caller aborts and enforces a 60-second overall deadline (retries included).
  Using a local controller instead of `AbortSignal.any` keeps compatibility with
  Node.js `>=20.0.0`, avoids a type-assertion workaround, and lets `finally`
  clear the timer and caller listener without spuriously aborting the request
  signal on normal completion. Already-aborted caller signals return cancelled
  immediately. The call is awaited through a small new helper, `abortable()`, so
  the tool returns at the deadline even if a lower layer does not react to the
  signal.
  - Timeout: `WEB_SEARCH_FAILED` with
    `Web search for query "…" timed out after 60 seconds.`
  - Cancellation by the user is unchanged (`Web search was cancelled.`) and
    takes precedence over a timeout.
- `core/client.ts`: `GeminiClient.generateContent` now passes its abort signal
  to `retryWithBackoff`, as `BaseLlmClient` does since #30182. Without this, an
  aborted call keeps retrying in the background: with only the time limit in
  place, another request was sent 25 s after the tool had returned.
- `utils/abortable.ts`: the new helper, with tests.
- The limit is a file-local constant for now. Making it configurable can follow,
  possibly together with #30212. `web_fetch` waits in the same way and is not
  changed here.

## Related Issues

Related to #29594

## How to Validate

1. Unit tests:

   ```bash
   npm test -w @google/gemini-cli-core -- src/tools/web-search.test.ts \
     src/utils/abortable.test.ts src/core/client.test.ts
   ```

2. End to end, against a local fake API. It makes the model call
   `google_web_search`, then never answers the search (`MODE=stall`) or answers
   every attempt with a 503 (`MODE=503`). The script is at the end of this
   section.

   ```bash
   # Terminal 1
   MODE=stall node fake-gemini-api.mjs

   # Terminal 2: after `npm run build`, from an empty directory
   mkdir -p /tmp/repro-home/.gemini
   echo '{"security":{"auth":{"selectedType":"gemini-api-key"}}}' \
     > /tmp/repro-home/.gemini/settings.json
   cd "$(mktemp -d)"
   HOME=/tmp/repro-home GEMINI_API_KEY=dummy \
     GOOGLE_GEMINI_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8787 \
     GEMINI_API_HEADERS_TIMEOUT_MS=86400000 \
     node /path/to/gemini-cli/packages/cli --skip-trust --approval-mode=yolo \
     -o stream-json -p "search the web for anything"
   ```

   | Mode    | Before                              | After                                                                        |
   | ------- | ----------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
   | `stall` | The command never finishes          | A `tool_result` with "timed out after 60 seconds" at 60 s, then the run ends |
   | `503`   | The search runs for several minutes | The same error at 60 s; the fake API gets no more requests after that        |

   Why raise `GEMINI_API_HEADERS_TIMEOUT_MS` in the API-key repro? On the Code
   Assist / Google sign-in path (`gaxios`), the undici headers timeout does not
   apply at all; and even on the API-key path, `UND_ERR_HEADERS_TIMEOUT` is in
   `RETRYABLE_NETWORK_CODES` (`utils/retry.ts`), so without an overall tool
   deadline a stalling endpoint is still retried up to 10 times.

   <details>
   <summary>fake-gemini-api.mjs</summary>

   ```js
   // Fake Gemini API for reproducing the web search hang in Gemini CLI.
   //
   // Usage: MODE=stall|503|ok node fake-gemini-api.mjs   (PORT=8787 by default)
   //   stall  the search request is never answered
   //   503    every search attempt gets a 503 error
   //   ok     the search is answered at once
   //
   // The first chat turn asks the CLI to run `google_web_search`. Once the tool
   // result comes back, the next chat turn replies "done".
   import http from 'node:http';

   const MODE = process.env.MODE || 'stall';
   const PORT = Number(process.env.PORT || 8787);
   let searchRequests = 0;

   const answer = (part, extra = {}) => ({
     candidates: [
       { content: { role: 'model', parts: [part] }, finishReason: 'STOP', ...extra },
     ],
     usageMetadata: { promptTokenCount: 1, candidatesTokenCount: 1, totalTokenCount: 2 },
   });

   http
     .createServer((req, res) => {
       let body = '';
       req.on('data', (chunk) => (body += chunk));
       req.on('end', () => {
         const now = new Date().toISOString();
         let json = {};
         try {
           json = JSON.parse(body);
         } catch {
           // Not JSON: handled as a helper call below.
         }
         const send = (status, payload, stream = false) => {
           res.writeHead(status, {
             'content-type': stream ? 'text/event-stream' : 'application/json',
           });
           res.end(
             stream ? `data: ${JSON.stringify(payload)}\r\n\r\n` : JSON.stringify(payload),
           );
         };

         if (req.url.includes(':streamGenerateContent')) {
           // Main chat turn. Check the parsed request, not the raw text: the
           // system prompt itself contains the word "functionResponse".
           const parts = (json.contents ?? []).flatMap((c) => c.parts ?? []);
           const searched = parts.some((p) => p.functionResponse);
           console.log(now, 'chat turn ->', searched ? 'text' : 'google_web_search');
           const part = searched
             ? { text: 'done' }
             : { functionCall: { name: 'google_web_search', args: { query: 'repro' } } };
           send(200, answer(part), true);
         } else if ((json.tools ?? []).some((tool) => tool.googleSearch)) {
           // The web search request itself.
           console.log(now, `web-search request #${++searchRequests} (${MODE})`);
           if (MODE === '503') {
             send(503, { error: { code: 503, message: 'unavailable', status: 'UNAVAILABLE' } });
           } else if (MODE === 'ok') {
             send(
               200,
               answer(
                 { text: 'Example result text.' },
                 {
                   groundingMetadata: {
                     groundingChunks: [{ web: { uri: 'https://example.com', title: 'Example' } }],
                     groundingSupports: [
                       { segment: { startIndex: 0, endIndex: 7 }, groundingChunkIndices: [0] },
                     ],
                   },
                 },
               ),
             );
           }
           // MODE=stall: never answer.
         } else {
           // Other helper calls. The reply answers the model-routing question;
           // any other helper ignores it.
           console.log(now, 'other', req.method, req.url);
           const routing = { complexity_reasoning: 'fake API', complexity_score: 50 };
           send(200, answer({ text: JSON.stringify(routing) }));
         }
       });
     })
     .listen(PORT, '127.0.0.1', () =>
       console.log(`fake Gemini API on http://127.0.0.1:${PORT} (MODE=${MODE})`),
     );
   ```

   </details>

## Pre-Merge Checklist

- [x] Updated relevant documentation and README (if needed)
- [x] Added/updated tests (if needed)
- [x] Noted breaking changes (if any)
- [ ] Validated on required platforms/methods:
  - [ ] MacOS
    - [x] npm run
    - [ ] npx
    - [ ] Docker
    - [ ] Podman
    - [ ] Seatbelt
  - [ ] Windows
    - [ ] npm run
    - [ ] npx
    - [ ] Docker
  - [ ] Linux
    - [ ] npm run
    - [ ] npx
    - [ ] Docker

issues.body:
### Feature hasn't been suggested before.

- [x] I have verified this feature I'm about to request hasn't been suggested before.

### Describe the enhancement you want to request

We’re exploring OpenCode v2 support for [Fly.io Sprites](https://sprites.dev/), where each OpenCode workspace would run inside a remote Sprite.

The new `WorkspaceDriver` abstraction appears to be a strong fit: a provider can provision a workspace, connect it to an environment driver, suspend it when idle, and destroy it. This would let OpenCode route shell and filesystem operations directly to a remote sandbox.

The current challenge is registration. `workspaceProviders` can be supplied when creating an embedded Effect SDK host, but it is not exposed through the v2 plugin API, and the Promise SDK omits that host option. As a result, third-party workspace drivers currently require replacing or custom-embedding the OpenCode host.

Would you consider exposing a supported extension point for registering workspace providers from packages or plugins? For example:
```
export default Plugin.define({
  id: "sprites",
  setup(ctx) {
    ctx.workspace.register("sprites", spritesWorkspaceDriver)
  },
})
```

Alternatively, workspace providers could be loaded from a dedicated configuration section:
```
{
  "workspaceProviders": {
    "sprites": {
      "package": "@flydotio/opencode-sprites"
    }
  }
}
```

The provider would implement the existing lifecycle contract:
```
interface WorkspaceDriver {
  create(input): Promise<{ binding: unknown }>
  connect(input): Promise<EnvironmentDriver>
  suspendForIdle(input): Promise<void>
  destroy(input): Promise<void>
}
```
This would enable Sprites and other remote sandbox systems—containers, VMs, cloud development environments, and ephemeral CI environments—without requiring each provider to ship a custom OpenCode server.

We’d be happy to prototype the Sprites driver and provide feedback on the extension API. Is package-level `WorkspaceDriver` registration something you would be open to supporting?

## Summary

An extension can return a `compaction` draft from a boundary handler (`agent_before_settle` / `turn_end`). This is the typed route for compaction during a run (`firstKeptEntryId: string | null`). Pi persists it as a normal compaction entry, but it emits none of the events the other compaction paths emit. Extensions that react to compaction through `session_compact` never learn that the context was compacted. For example, a memory extension that archives the summary and injects a recovery notice misses it.

## Versions

- `@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent` 1.6.1
- macOS

## Where

In `dist/core/agent-session.js` (1.6.1):

- `_applyBoundaryDrafts` (~L637-656): a `compaction` draft goes to `manager.appendCompaction(summary, firstKeptEntryId, tokensBefore, details, true, usage)` (~L652-653). No event is emitted afterwards.
- `compact()` emits `session_compact` (~L2410) and `compaction_end` (~L2428/~L2442) after appending its entry.
- Auto-compaction emits `session_compact` (~L2699) and `compaction_end` (~L2714).

So the same kind of entry (`type: "compaction"`) is announced on two paths and silent on the third.

## Why the boundary path matters

`ctx.compact()` is fire-and-forget. In print mode (`pi -p`) the runtime is disposed as soon as `prompt()` resolves, and `dispose()` aborts a compaction that is still running ("Compaction cancelled"). Any follow-up message sent from `onComplete` never runs. A boundary draft is awaited inside the prompt, so it is the only route that works in every mode. Extensions that use it must currently choose: either work in print/JSON mode, or let `session_compact` listeners see the compaction.

## Reproduction (verified on 1.6.1)

`a.ts` returns a compaction draft from the settle boundary:
```ts
export default function (pi: any) {
	let done = false;
	pi.on("agent_before_settle", (event: any) => {
		if (done || event.outcome !== "completed") return;
		done = true;
		return { entries: [...event.entries, { type: "compaction", summary: "S", firstKeptEntryId: null }] };
	});
}
```
`b.ts` logs the compaction events it observes:
```ts
import { appendFileSync } from "node:fs";
const log = (line: string) => appendFileSync(process.env.EVENTS_LOG!, line + "\n");
export default function (pi: any) {
	pi.on("session_before_compact", (e: any) => void log(`session_before_compact reason=${e.reason}`));
	pi.on("session_compact", (e: any) => void log(`session_compact reason=${e.reason}`));
}
```
`c.ts` (control) compacts through `ctx.compact`, with a summary built in the hook so no LLM call is needed:
```ts
export default function (pi: any) {
	let done = false;
	pi.on("session_before_compact", () => ({ compaction: { summary: "S", firstKeptEntryId: null, tokensBefore: 0 } }));
	pi.on("agent_settled", (_e: any, ctx: any) => {
		if (done) return;
		done = true;
		ctx.compact({ onComplete: () => {}, onError: (err: Error) => console.error(err.message) });
	});
}
```
Runs:
```sh
EVENTS_LOG=$PWD/events-boundary.log pi --no-extensions -e a.ts -e b.ts -p --session-dir sessions-boundary 'Reply with the single word hi.'
# control: the session must exceed keepRecentTokens, so four 3000-line files are read first
EVENTS_LOG=$PWD/events-control.log pi --no-extensions -e c.ts -e b.ts -p --session-dir sessions-control \
  'Run `cat big1.txt`, `cat big2.txt`, `cat big3.txt`, `cat big4.txt`, each in its own tool call. Then reply with the single word hi.'
```

Results:

| Run | Session entries end with | Events logged by `b.ts` |
|---|---|---|
| boundary draft (`a.ts`) | `compaction` | none |
| control (`c.ts`, `ctx.compact`) | `compaction` | `session_before_compact reason=manual`, `session_compact reason=manual` |

The same kind of entry is persisted in both runs, but only the control run announces it.

## Expected

After persisting a boundary `compaction` draft, emit `session_compact` with the same payload shape as the other paths: the persisted compaction entry and a reason, for example `"extension"` or the boundary name. Emitting `compaction_start`/`compaction_end` for consistency would help UIs too. If emitting it from the boundary commit is undesirable, a documented alternative event would also work.

## Context

Found while building pi-compactor (a phased compaction extension: preflight handoff, safe points, pins). Today it uses `ctx.compact` in tui/rpc mode, so `session_compact` listeners work there, and boundary drafts in print/json mode, where they don't. With the event emitted for boundary drafts, it could use one route everywhere.

### What happened?

On Pi 1.6.0, `/new` briefly shows the built-in footer before the custom footer returns. I reproduced it in regular and fullscreen modes with only the extension below. The same behavior was [reported in a comment on the related header issue #11025](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/issues/11025#issuecomment-6211067389).

### Steps to reproduce

Save as `footer-repro.mjs`:

```js
export default function (pi) {
  pi.on("session_start", ({ reason }, ctx) => {
    ctx.ui.setFooter(() => ({
      invalidate() {},
      render: () => [`CUSTOM FOOTER (${reason})`, ""],
    }));
  });
}
```

Run `pi --offline -ne --no-session -e ./footer-repro.mjs`, then type `/new`.

### Expected behavior

Switch directly to the new custom footer. I have a tested patch that keeps the last rendered rows visible without keeping the old extension alive. Happy to prepare a PR.

### Version

1.6.0

## Summary

A `/reload` of unchanged code can change the system prompt's tool list order, so the next Anthropic request misses the prompt cache. Nothing before the tool definitions is reused, and Anthropic drops the earlier thinking blocks with `prefix_binding_mismatch`. The `tools` array is not affected: it follows the transcript's declared order (`getCurrentTools`), while `<tools>` and the guideline lines follow the active-tool order.

## Cause

`_applyToolLoadout` (`packages/coding-agent/src/core/agent-session.ts:1671-1674` at b882d08) orders the loadout as "already active, then newly requested". On reload, `reload()` keeps the previous loadout as pending names, but only tools that are already registered are activated. A tool that an extension registers late keeps its place in the transcript, but it is appended to the active order. Example: a tool registered in `session_start` after an `await`. A tool registered at load time stays where it was, so the late tool now comes after it. Any `setActiveTools()` call that deactivates a tool also clears the pending names (`setActiveToolsByName`). `_rebuildSystemPrompt` then renders `<tools>` and the guidelines in that new order. The sections differ from the transcript, so a new system message is recorded and the prompt prefix changes. The same applies on resume (`_restoreToolsFromTranscript`).

Seen with pi 1.5.2 in the TUI: `codemode` is registered at load and activated by the MCP extension at startup. An extension's `ask_user` tools are registered in `session_start` after an await. Before the reload the list is `ask_user, ask_user_resolve, ask_user_adopt, codemode`; after it, `codemode, ask_user, ...`.

## Repro (SDK)

1. Write a transcript whose system message declares `[..., "late_tool", "early_tool"]`.
2. Extension A registers `early_tool` at load. Extension B registers `late_tool` in `session_start` after `await setTimeout(10)`. Both tools have a `promptSnippet`.
3. Run `createAgentSession`, then `bindExtensions(...)`. `session.systemPrompt` lists `early_tool` before `late_tool`, which does not match the declared order. A `reload()` gives the same order.

## Proposed fix

Keep tools the transcript already declares in their declared order. Tools it does not declare yet follow, in their current order:

```diff
 	private _applyToolLoadout(toolNames: string[]): AgentTool[] {
 		const requested = new Set(toolNames);
 		const kept = this.getActiveToolNames().filter((name) => requested.has(name));
-		const tools = [...new Set([...kept, ...requested])].flatMap((name) => {
+		// Tools the transcript already declares keep their declared order, so a reload or resume that
+		// registers them in another order renders the same prompt sections.
+		const declared = getCurrentTools(this.agent.state.messages).map((tool) => tool.name);
+		const rank = (name: string) => {
+			const index = declared.indexOf(name);
+			return index < 0 ? declared.length : index;
+		};
+		const ordered = [...new Set([...kept, ...requested])]
+			.map((name, index) => [name, index] as const)
+			.sort((a, b) => rank(a[0]) - rank(b[0]) || a[1] - b[1])
+			.map(([name]) => name);
+		const tools = ordered.flatMap((name) => {
```

This keeps #11495's guarantee: reordering the same names still does not change the prompt.

## Measured (pi 1.5.2, TUI, about 118k context, reload of identical code)

| | system field | first request after the reload: cacheRead / cacheWrite |
|---|---|---|
| unpatched | `<tools>` and guidelines reordered | 35,057 / 83,089 (30%) |
| patched | byte-identical | 116,383 / 1,183 (99%) |

**What happens**

On Linux, the host exec memory guard (`src/process/child-memory.ts`, `readMemory()` + `readTreeMemory()`) reads `VmRSS`/`VmSwap` from `/proc/<pid>/status` for every process in a command's tree and adds them up. RSS includes shared pages (shared libraries, zygote-forked children, shmem), so the same pages are charged once per process that maps them.

Multi-process programs are hit hardest. `google-chrome --headless=new` starts ~14–15 processes that share most of their memory. On a gateway with `MemoryMax=4G` the shared budget is about 1.8 GiB in 2026.11.10 (1 GiB per command in 2026.11.7). Chrome gets killed with "Command killed because the shared host exec memory budget was exceeded" while it actually uses a fraction of that.

**Numbers (Ubuntu 24.04, kernel 6.x)**

- Headless Chrome, 14 processes, running ~30 h: sum of VmRSS 1028 MiB, sum of Pss 331 MiB (3.1×).
- Fresh `google-chrome --headless=new about:blank` after 0.9 s: 15 processes, VmRSS sum 1142 MiB, Pss sum 261 MiB. Killed after ~1 s on a 1024 MiB limit.
- Synthetic repro (Docker, `--memory=1g`, budget 640 MiB): a Python process touches 200 MiB and forks 5 idle children. VmRSS sum 1233 MiB, Pss sum 208 MiB. 2026.11.10 kills it. A process with 900 MiB of private memory is killed either way, as it should be.

**Expected**

Shared pages should be charged proportionally, not once per process.

**Proposed fix**

Read `Pss` and `SwapPss` from `/proc/<pid>/smaps_rollup` (Linux ≥ 4.14). Fall back to the current `VmRSS`/`VmSwap` from `status` when `smaps_rollup` can't be read (older kernels, ptrace-restricted processes) or has no `Pss` line. The budget formula stays the same. Only the per-process usage changes:

```ts
const pssField = Buffer.from("\nPss:");
const swapPssField = Buffer.from("\nSwapPss:");

async function readMemory(pid: number): Promise<HostExecMemoryUsage> {
  try {
    const rollup = await readKernelFile(`/proc/${pid}/smaps_rollup`);
    if (rollup.indexOf(pssField) >= 0) {
      return {
        memoryBytes: readStatusNumber(rollup, pssField) * 1024,
        swapBytes: readStatusNumber(rollup, swapPssField) * 1024,
      };
    }
  } catch {
    // Older kernels or ptrace-restricted processes: fall back to status.
  }
  const status = await readKernelFile(`/proc/${pid}/status`);
  return {
    memoryBytes: readStatusNumber(status, rssField) * 1024,
    swapBytes: readStatusNumber(status, swapField) * 1024,
  };
}
```

Tests: one case where four processes each report `VmRSS: 96 kB` but `Pss: 24 kB` stays under a 128 kB budget until one grows privately, and a fallback case for EACCES/EPERM/ENOENT on `smaps_rollup`. All existing `child-memory.test.ts` cases pass. One of them needs the injected EACCES to also hit `smaps_rollup`, otherwise the fallback hides the error it checks.

Notes:
- `smaps_rollup` is a bit more expensive to read than `status` (it walks the VMAs under the mmap lock). The guard samples every 1–5 s, so in practice this doesn't matter.
- Pss also splits pages shared with processes outside the tree (e.g. libc with the gateway), so the tree is charged slightly less than its exclusive footprint. That error is far smaller than counting shared pages N times.
- There is currently no configuration key to raise or disable the budget (`tools/exec.md`: "No configuration is required"), so affected users can only work around it by starting heavy multi-process tools outside the exec tree.

### Summary

On `qwen-token-plan/qwen4-max`, the Qwen provider wrapper turns thinking on for both `low` and `xhigh` but sends no top-level `reasoning_effort`. Turning thinking off still works.

### Steps to reproduce

With `@openclaw/qwen-provider` 2026.10.34 loaded, run the offline reproducer below. It passes explicit thinking levels to the released plugin and captures the payload after its `onPayload` hook. No API key, network inference, or session data is needed. The Qwen3.8 run is a positive control.

<details><summary>Runnable reproduction (shell, Node.js and jq)</summary>

```sh
set -e
export QWEN_PLUGIN_ROOT="$(openclaw plugins inspect qwen --json | jq -er '.plugin.rootDir')"
node --input-type=module <<'JS'
import { pathToFileURL } from 'node:url';
const file = pathToFileURL(process.env.QWEN_PLUGIN_ROOT + '/dist/stream.js');
const { wrapQwenProviderStream } = await import(file);
for (const [id, levels] of [['qwen4-max', ['off','low','medium','high','xhigh']], ['qwen3.8-max', ['low','high']]]) {
  const model = { id, provider: 'qwen-token-plan', api: 'openai-completions', reasoning: true };
  for (const thinkingLevel of levels) {
    const capture = async (m, _, opts) => {
      const body = { model: m.id, messages: [] };
      const sent = (await opts.onPayload(body, m)) ?? body;
      console.log(m.id, thinkingLevel, sent.enable_thinking, sent.reasoning_effort ?? '(absent)');
    };
    const run = wrapQwenProviderStream({ provider: model.provider, modelId: id, model, sourceApi: model.api, thinkingLevel, streamFn: capture });
    await run(model, { messages: [] }, {});
  }
}
JS
```

</details>

### Expected behavior

The [Qwen4-Max API reference](https://help.aliyun.com/zh/model-studio/qwen4-max) documents `low`, `high`, and `xhigh` for Chat Completions (default: `high`). An explicit `low` or `xhigh` should send that value as `reasoning_effort`; `off` should disable thinking without sending effort. The model’s middle tier is `high`, not Qwen3.8’s `medium`.

### Actual behavior

| Model | Selected level | `enable_thinking` | `reasoning_effort` |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `qwen4-max` | `off` | `false` | absent |
| `qwen4-max` | `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh` (tested separately) | `true` | absent for all four |
| `qwen3.8-max` (control) | `low` | `true` | `low` |
| `qwen3.8-max` (control) | `high` | `true` | `xhigh` |

### Environment

OpenClaw `2026.10.34` (`c50d7cc`), `@openclaw/qwen-provider` `2026.10.34`, macOS 27.2.1 arm64, Node.js 24.22.1. Route: `qwen-token-plan/qwen4-max`, `openai-completions`, `reasoning: true`; no custom thinking format or budget.

### Impact and scope

Users cannot request lower or higher effort on this route: neither non-default choice reaches the service. The observed evidence is the plugin output, not a latency, billing, or server-side measurement.

The same decision is visible in the `v2026.11.10` source: [model family selection](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/v2026.11.10/extensions/qwen/stream.ts#L105-L125) and [fallback deletes `reasoning_effort`](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/v2026.11.10/extensions/qwen/stream.ts#L383-L413). That version was inspected, not runtime-tested here.

