## What Problem This Solves

An authorized inbound FaceTime call can be answered before OpenClaw's realtime voice path is ready. Callers hear silence and may start speaking before the assistant can respond.

Fixes #202917

## Why This Change Was Made

The FaceTime event handler now waits for the talk driver's existing provider and route readiness before answering, then checks that the call is still active. It keeps the current muted-answer and audio-activation sequence.

## User Impact

Inbound FaceTime calls keep ringing until OpenClaw is ready to speak, reducing silent openings and repeated greetings.

## Evidence

- Production event-handler boundary, pinned base `52cc86028d703a4820b688e726097842d05d2155` vs tested head `b7eee9cd62b6307fe258d016e651493d31433084`:

  ```text
  base: readiness-pending answerCalls=1; order=suppression-ready, answer-muted, provider-and-route-readiness
  head: readiness-pending answerCalls=0; order=suppression-ready, provider-and-route-readiness
  head after readiness: answerCalls=1; answer-muted then activate
  negative control: unauthorized incoming call was not answered
  ```

  The same `node scripts/test-projects.mts extensions/facetime/tests/runtime-production-proof.test.ts` harness ran on both versions. It calls the changed production event handler with a fixed inbound event and controlled helper/media boundaries. No live FaceTime call was placed or received.
- Focused regression: `node scripts/test-projects.mts extensions/facetime/tests/runtime.test.ts` passed all 25 tests, including the deferred-readiness ordering case.
- Existing `readyForAudio()` is the talk driver's readiness contract; `activateCallTalk()` already waits on it before enabling media.

<details>
<summary>Production event-handler proof source</summary>

Save this as `extensions/facetime/tests/runtime-production-proof.test.ts` and run the command above from the repository root:

```ts
import { expect, it } from "vitest";
import { resolveFaceTimeConfig } from "../src/config.js";
import { createFaceTimeCallEventHandler } from "../src/runtime-call-events.js";
import { FaceTimeCallRegistry } from "../src/runtime-state.js";
import { incomingCall } from "./fixtures.test-support.js";

it("shows the production event handler waiting before answer", async () => {
  const calls = new FaceTimeCallRegistry();
  const order: string[] = [];
  let releaseReadiness = () => {};
  let readinessEntered = () => {};
  const readiness = new Promise<void>((resolve) => {
    releaseReadiness = resolve;
  });
  const entered = new Promise<void>((resolve) => {
    readinessEntered = resolve;
  });
  let readinessCalls = 0;
  let answerCalls = 0;
  const answerResult = {
    outcome: "answered-muted",
    muted: true,
    is_uplink_muted: true,
    helpersContacted: 1,
    topologyGeneration: 1,
    topologyComplete: true,
    helperResults: [{ outcome: "answered-muted", muted: true, is_uplink_muted: true }],
  };
  const handler = createFaceTimeCallEventHandler({
    calls,
    helper: {
      answerCall: async () => {
        answerCalls += 1;
        order.push("answer-muted");
        return answerResult;
      },
    } as never,
    config: resolveFaceTimeConfig({ ownerHandles: ["owner@example.com"] }),
    logger: { info() {}, warn() {}, debug() {}, error() {} } as never,
    callControl: {
      startCallTalk: async (call) => {
        order.push("suppression-ready");
        call.talk = {
          readyForAudio: async () => {
            readinessCalls += 1;
            if (readinessCalls === 1) {
              order.push("provider-and-route-readiness");
              readinessEntered();
            }
            await readiness;
          },
        } as never;
      },
      activateCallTalk: async (call) => {
        await call.talk?.readyForAudio();
        order.push("activate");
      },
      closeCall: async () => {},
      attemptCarrierHangup: async () => {},
    } as never,
    isStopping: () => false,
    isDriverInstallPending: () => false,
    getPendingDial: () => undefined,
    clearPendingDial: async () => {},
    persistPendingDial: async () => {},
    outboundCarrierPeers: new Map(),
    cancelPendingDial: async () => {},
  });

  const processing = handler.handleCallEvent(incomingCall() as never);
  await entered;
  console.log(JSON.stringify({ phase: "readiness-pending", answerCalls, order }));
  releaseReadiness();
  await processing;
  console.log(JSON.stringify({ phase: "ready", answerCalls, order }));
  expect(answerCalls).toBe(1);

  const unauthorized = createFaceTimeCallEventHandler({
    calls: new FaceTimeCallRegistry(),
    helper: { answerCall: async () => { throw new Error("unexpected answer"); } } as never,
    config: resolveFaceTimeConfig({ ownerHandles: ["owner@example.com"] }),
    logger: { info() {}, warn() {}, debug() {}, error() {} } as never,
    callControl: {} as never,
    isStopping: () => false,
    isDriverInstallPending: () => false,
    getPendingDial: () => undefined,
    clearPendingDial: async () => {},
    persistPendingDial: async () => {},
    outboundCarrierPeers: new Map(),
    cancelPendingDial: async () => {},
  });
  await unauthorized.handleCallEvent(
    incomingCall(4, { handle: { value: "other@example.com" } }) as never,
  );
  console.log("negative-control: unauthorized incoming call was not answered");
});
```

</details>

AI-assisted: built with Codex

Fixes #204738. Related: #203532, #153426. The preventive change in #204785 does not recover existing files.

## What Problem This Solves

Doctor asks operators to fix an excluded `MEMORY.md`, but an owner edit does not clear the provenance quarantine and Doctor cannot restore it. The file can remain absent from automatic context indefinitely.

## User Impact

On the Gateway host, stop the Gateway and run `openclaw memory trust --agent <id>`. Open the indicated file and review all its bytes. Rerun with `--sha256 <reviewed-digest>` to restore that file, not whatever happens to be at its path later. Doctor now points to the recovery. If you use the built-in search index, explicitly refresh it with `openclaw memory index --force --agent <id>` while offline; trust alone does not reclassify already-derived chunks or call embedding providers.

## Why This Change Was Made

**Product behavior proposed for the patch:** This is an offline, local state-owner CLI action for the configured workspace's root `MEMORY.md` only. It is not exposed to Gateway guests, agents, chat, or RPC. There is no automatic recovery, `--yes` shortcut, or override for a mismatching digest. The provenance change and latest restoration receipt commit together; the receipt records the CLI source, time, reviewed hash, and previous tracked write. Historical rows do not identify what originally quarantined them. Later unreviewed bytes are not eligible, and a later tainted tracked write still quarantines the file. This does not change the existing ratchet for other files.

## Evidence

- **Before:** built baseline `c734b2de9dd9`, disposable Gateway and local Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct. Two model-generated tracked writes around an external edit produced an `untrusted` record whose hash matched the file. A fresh turn's system prompt omitted the file, Doctor reported the exclusion, and the CLI had no `trust` command.
- **After:** candidate `351b47445436`, disposable Gateway and local Qwen3-1.7B through llama.cpp. The model emitted the tracked write calls and supplied their arguments; the loopback observer checked the actual outgoing system prompt, not the model's answer. The quarantined bytes were absent. A read-only operator had no restoration RPC, and the local CLI refused while the Gateway was online. Offline preview returned the matching digest without changing provenance; a stale digest was rejected after changing the reviewed file. The matching confirmation persisted `agent`, the reviewed hash, and a CLI audit with the previous `untrusted` write. Doctor stopped reporting that finding; the next reset turn included the reviewed bytes. A subsequent external edit was excluded. A third model-generated tracked write restored the quarantine, and a fresh turn again omitted the file. The current-head FTS check also confirmed existing chunks stayed `untrusted` after restore alone and switched to `agent` only after explicit refresh.
- **Stable state and derived index:** on the same disposable state created by packaged 2026.11.10, the initial candidate `de58009cada6` preserved quarantine. With deterministic loopback completions, restore and next-turn inclusion passed. Persisted FTS chunks stayed `untrusted` after trust alone and switched to `agent` only after forced index refresh. A subsequent tracked write after another external edit returned the row to `untrusted`, retained the trust receipt, and stayed outside fresh automatic context.
- **Checks:** 88 tests in eight focused files; targeted core/extension type checks, changed-TypeScript lint and format, two docs MDX checks, `git diff --check`, and the signed head's `pnpm build:runtime` passed.

- **Classifier follow-up:** [ClawSweeper flagged](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/205726#issuecomment-7181952186) a compatibility issue in the initial patch. The follow-up keeps local pinned-source metadata separate from the remote capability check. Source-aware classifiers and host fallback still use that metadata, but a legacy local classifier still has the final say on its own quarantine. The new bootstrap regression failed on the prior head (the selected classifier was not invoked) and passed on the fixed head. All 33 focused bootstrap/runtime tests and the exact-head runtime build passed.

- **CI test type correction:** The follow-up at `0512ae00cdec` preserves the fixture's `untrusted` literal type. The failing core test-type configuration now passes locally. No production file changed from the Qwen3-proven runtime head above.

