Closes #205939

## What Problem This Solves

Shipped 2026.9.37 Git updaters launch the target inspection worker while holding a maintenance lease. Ordinary shared-state plugin discovery rejects that lease and aborts database capture.

## User Impact

I fix this on the target side without changing the shipped updater, schemas, or foreign-writer protection. Legacy discovery reads private state, preserves the source DB/WAL/SHM bytes, and still discovers retained plugin stores whose installation records exist only in WAL.

## Why This Change Was Made

I feed plugin metadata from the shared inspection already owned by schema discovery, inventory, or snapshot capture into the existing scoped plugin cache. The separate metadata copy is removed, so it cannot allocate outside scratch, mask the original read error, or copy a sparse 20 GiB file before capacity admission.

Unprepared legacy requests default to source-preserving snapshots. Current launchers send their existing read policy explicitly, preserving prepared-inventory and online-writer behavior. The existing snapshot owner retains its cleanup and capacity policy.

The simplify pass removed the independent metadata acquisition rather than adding cleanup exceptions or capacity bypasses. Net delta against the base: +79 production lines and +193 test lines.

## Evidence

### Installed Git-update proof (8931f9b)

I reviewed the supplied redacted captures for head `8931f9b5785d677a37818dcdb00bdd78a202c392`. Installed Git updates in fresh Linux VM fixtures passed from all three releases:

| Starting release | Update result | Verified rollback images |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 2026.9.37 (`81da850`) | Exit 0, status `ok`, ledger `succeeded` | 4, including both retained plugin stores |
| 2026.10.34 (`c50d7cc`) | Exit 0, status `ok`, ledger `succeeded` | Exactly 2: shared and main-agent databases, both integrity OK |
| 2026.11.10 (`6ea542f`) | Exit 0, status `ok`, ledger `succeeded` | 4, including both retained plugin stores |

Every systemd Gateway activated and served `8931f9b`; settings, application data, and both disabled-plugin account stores were preserved, with matching row digests and integrity OK. The 10.34 updater does not capture plugin rollback images; its live plugin rows and installed plugin files were preserved. Version 2026.11.10 was latest stable when checked against npm and GitHub at 20:17 UTC on November 26.

The first 10.34 attempt stopped before activation at the candidate build's heap guard: 3813 MB resolved versus 4352 MB required, with zero OOM kills. The same 8-GiB VM, fixture, and checks passed with `OPENCLAW_TSDOWN_MAX_OLD_SPACE_MB=5120`; this was a build-resource failure, not a maintenance-lease failure.

**WAL preservation:** for each release, a separate probe replayed the legacy discovery request with the installed target worker and plugin registration present only in committed WAL. Each exited 0, discovered both plugin stores, and left source DB, WAL, and SHM sizes and SHA-256 values byte-identical. The 9.37 and 11.10 probes used private copies of verified update snapshots. The 10.34 probe used its two verified rollback images plus two independently checksum-verified plugin fixtures copied before the update. These measurements bracket the separate discovery probes, not the live updates; no matching live-update subprocess sample was captured. The supplied reports confirm temporary VM cleanup, and the 10.34 report also confirms scratch cleanup.

